- densify deposited films;
- change film structure;
- improve interfaces;
- form low-resistance contacts;
- reduce contamination or unwanted defects.

So an anneal tool is not tied to one single transistor step.

Different recipes appear throughout the flow.

This creates a broad installed base, but it also means competition varies by application.

13. The economic tradeoff: batch versus single wafer

Suppose a batch furnace processes many wafers together.

That can produce excellent throughput and attractive cost per wafer.

But suppose an advanced transistor step requires an extremely narrow time-temperature window.

A single-wafer RTP tool may cost more per chamber and process fewer wafers simultaneously, but it can provide much tighter control.

The fab cares about the economics of good output, not merely the number of wafers that fit inside the machine.

So the comparison is:

lower equipment cost or higher batch productivity

versus

better process control, lower diffusion, improved uniformity and potentially higher yield.

This is the same economic framework we have repeatedly used.

Cost per tool is not the final metric.

Cost per good wafer is closer to what matters.

14. Throughput versus process quality

Thermal tools also show why throughput cannot be viewed alone.

A faster thermal cycle can raise wafers per hour.

But if the temperature profile is less uniform, electrical variation may rise and yield may fall.

A fab therefore optimizes several variables at once:

Throughput = wafers processed per unit time.

Uniformity = how consistently the thermal result is achieved across the wafer.

Repeatability = how consistently one wafer matches the next.

Yield = how much final output becomes sellable product.

Cost of ownership = the total economic cost of running the tool.

The highest-throughput machine is not automatically the best machine.

15. Why thermal processing becomes harder as chips scale

At first glance, annealing sounds like an old, mature process.

But scaling makes the control problem harder.

Smaller features mean:

- dopants have less room to diffuse;
- interfaces are thinner and more sensitive;
- tiny electrical variations matter more;
- new materials respond differently to heat;
- three-dimensional structures create more complex temperature behavior;
- later process steps must avoid damaging structures already built.

So the process category may be old while the technical challenge keeps changing.

This is a useful investing pattern.

Do not ask only whether a process is new.

Ask whether the tolerance window is shrinking.

Shrinking tolerance can increase the value of precision equipment.

16. Why this matters for AI chips

Advanced AI accelerators depend on leading-edge logic.

Leading-edge logic depends on extremely controlled transistor formation.

Thermal processing is one of the steps that makes those transistors electrically usable and repeatable.

But thermal equipment is not an AI-only market.

The same vendors sell into memory, mature logic, analog, power devices and other semiconductor categories.

That means AI can increase leading-edge demand without making thermal-processing revenue move one-for-one with NVIDIA GPU shipments.

The investment read-through is indirect:
